meta {
  name: Review Audit
  type: http
  seq: 9
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/deal-audits/deals/{{auditDealId}}/review
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{auditReviewerToken}}
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "decision": "request_changes",
    "reasonCodes": ["Missing Docs"],
    "notes": "Declarations page still outstanding.",
    "score": 70
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 201 Created", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(201);
  });

  test("lands on a state matching the decision", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(["Pass", "Fail", "Not Submitted"]).to.include(body.auditStatus);
    expect(body).to.have.property("reviewedAt");
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /deal-audits/deals/:dealId/review — the reviewer's verdict (PAC-72)

  - **Module:** `deal_audits`
  - **Permission:** `deal_audits:write`
  - **Data scope:** assignee **or** reviewer (see **Set Audit Assignment**).

  Only an audit in **Pending** can be reviewed — anything else is `409`.

  ## Decisions
  | decision          | lands on      | meaning |
  |-------------------|---------------|---------|
  | `approve`         | `Pass`        | signed off |
  | `request_changes` | `Fail`        | this failed review; here is what is wrong |
  | `send_back`       | `Not Submitted` | not ready / not mine — take it back |

  The last two both return the audit to its assignee and differ only in
  meaning. They stay separate so the timeline can say which happened
  (`audit_changes_requested` vs `audit_sent_back`).

  > 🔴 **The submitter cannot review their own audit** — `403`. Both actions are
  > `deal_audits:write` and the Producer role template holds it, so nothing else
  > in the guard chain expresses "a different person". This is the rule that
  > makes the two-role split mean anything.

  ## Body
  | field       | type     | rules |
  |-------------|----------|-------|
  | decision    | enum     | required — `approve` \| `request_changes` \| `send_back` |
  | reasonCodes | string[] | **required (≥1) when `request_changes`**; ignored on approve |
  | notes       | string   | ≤ 2000 chars |
  | score       | number   | integer 0–100 |

  `reasonCodes` vocabulary (SmartSuite's own): `Missing Docs`,
  `Coverage Not Offered`, `Incorrect Named Insured`, `Incorrect Address`,
  `Underwriting Issue`, `Other`.

  Requiring a reason on a failure is the point of the field: "it failed" with
  nothing stated gives the assignee nothing to act on and the correction loop
  just bounces.

  **Reason codes are cleared on any non-failure outcome** — stale codes on a
  passed audit would read as "passed, but here's what was wrong with it".

  ## Response `201`
  The full audit — same shape as **Set Audit Assignment**.

  ## Errors
  - `400 Bad Request` — unknown decision, or `request_changes` with no reason code.
  - `403 Forbidden` — missing permission, or the caller submitted this audit.
  - `404 Not Found` — no audit for that deal, or out of reach.
  - `409 Conflict` — the audit is not awaiting review.
}
